dep, versions: one-atom match path, slot reads for Atom attributes, unbounded version caches

Sub-series: H (atom matching caches) - patch 1 of 1; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.

match_from_list() with one plain Atom and a one-element list is the
resolver's most frequent match. A short path (_match_single) performs the
same tests in the same order for the common atom kinds (no operator, or
> >= < <=, with or without slot) and leaves blockers, wildcards, repo
atoms, = =* ~ and USE dependencies to the original code. The kind of an
atom is computed once and kept in a new slot (_match_kind).

vercmp() and catpkgsplit() get unbounded lru caches (they had 1,024 and
10,240 entries, fewer than the distinct arguments of one run). Both are
pure functions of their arguments.

Fifteen Atom properties that only return one slot (cp, cpv, slot,
operator, use, unevaluated_atom, ...) are replaced on the class by the slot
descriptors themselves, so reading them needs no Python call.

This is patch 0021 of the 25-patch ladder without its fourth piece, the
string-keyed atom caches in _emerge, which changes a line of patch 0002
and is now patch 0028 (sub-series A). The figures that follow are those of
the four pieces together, on top of patches 0001-0020, resolve seconds
before -> after (median of the runs, at least 2 per figure):
  start 25.7 -> 24.0, holds 12.9 -> 12.2, masks 27.2 -> 25.4,
  py313 15.8 -> 15.1, bdeps 18.5 -> 17.5, policykit 13.2 -> 12.4
  sum of six 113.3 -> 106.6 (-6.6 s, -1.5 % of the stock sum 447.5)
Alone on 0001-0009 none of the pieces was distinguishable from noise on
the start state. The three pieces kept here alone on stock 3.0.83: see
README (sub-series H).

Weak spots and what was not checked:
* A second implementation of part of match_from_list() that has to be kept
  in step with it.
* The two caches now grow without bound for the life of the process (a
  concern for long-running API users, not for emerge).
* The attributes become assignable: a property without a setter raised
  AttributeError on assignment, a slot descriptor does not. Whether any code
  assigns them was not searched.

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21; cut out of its
former patch on 2026-11-21 without changing a line of code. Not a
human-written change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on
six states of one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the
same merge list in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts
and the same package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
